From ca35cddd779aa3eb3cdc25745f65c0a4a0420c8c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Stephen Kent <43362477+kentstephen@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 17 Sep 2026 18:25:19 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 1/5] Read kerchunk JSON manifests (virtual Zarr) via
 format='kerchunk'

Adds ManifestStore, a ReadableStorageTraits implementation that serves a
kerchunk reference manifest: metadata documents and inline (plain or
base64) values from memory, and every other key as a byte-range read of
the referenced file through DuckDB's FileSystem API, with open handles
pooled across chunks. read_zarr, read_zarr_metadata and read_zarr_groups
take format='kerchunk' to select it. A missing key decodes to the fill
value, as for never-written HDF5 chunks.

Manifest details handled: spec versions 0 and 1, templates substitution,
gen rejected with a clear error, file:// paths, bare NaN/Infinity tokens
in metadata (Python json output) normalised to the quoted Zarr v2 forms,
virtual-tiff's imagecodecs_* codec ids aliased to their numcodecs names,
and a synthesised .zmetadata so the existing consolidated-metadata array
listing works unchanged. References are deserialised without building a
generic JSON tree and paths are shared between entries, since manifests
with hundreds of thousands of references are common.

Enables the zarrs zlib and fletcher32 features so the HDF5 filter
pipeline written by VirtualiZarr decodes. Checksum validation is skipped
on manifest reads: zarrs 0.23's fletcher32 drops the odd trailing byte
from its checksum, so odd-length payloads fail with a correct checksum.

Fixtures: a NetCDF4 file (shuffle + deflate + fletcher32, deflate only,
and a contiguous dataset) indexed by VirtualiZarr, two tiled GeoTIFFs
(Deflate float32 with nodata, 3-band ZSTD int8) indexed by virtual-tiff,
each with a Zarr v2 twin, and a hand-built manifest over Zarr v2 chunk
files with one chunk left out. test/sql/read_zarr_kerchunk.test asserts
every manifest read equals its twin in both directions and covers the
missing-chunk and error paths.

Closes #45.
